YANI: The Crud On The Floor Rises!

Another idea for promoting speedy play in the
mid-game, if you know what's good for you.

You know, we don't pay enough attention to all
those rotting corpses. Sure, the footing is a
bit treacherous, so those smooth soled "fumble
boots" might give you a bit of trouble, but still,
rotted corpses don't just _evaporate_, they merely
break down into unusable components.

But suppose that wasn't so? Suppose that the DoD
itself, slowly developed a dislike to adventurers
trespassing. Suppose it tried to gather its energies
together to "repel boarders", but couldn't quite
manage, just go closer and close to success with
time?

Now suppose you mix in the magical energies all
adventurers seem to have sparking off them from time
to time, and maybe the situation changes?

Suppose that a dungeon powered up over the course of
an adventure began to _react_ to all that energy
added by the adventurer?

So that, say, a fireball cast across a room risked
raising first burning spheres, later fire
elementals, all along its path and beyond?

So that, say, a healing spell had a greater and
greater chance of rousing first a few, then several,
then many trolls of increasing HD around the caster?

So that, say, a finger or wand of death risked
raising ever more powerful undead as time went on.

So that, eventually, even the mere passage of
enchanted armor began calling "beings from the vasty
deep" out of the dust bunny armies ready to spring
back to life at a moment's provocation?

YANI: Things Wear Out

As aging effects go, wouldn't, say, reflection, be a
lot more valuable if its effectiveness decayed over
time? Or magic resistence, or magic cancellation, or
_any_ intrinsics, if they were probabilities sliding
down a decay curve instead of binary switches?

You see some writing on the floor here. It says:
Congratulations, Sokoban conqueror. -- more --
Elbereth says: Enjoy your amulet of reflection -- more --
guaranteed for at least 30,000 turns or 300 zaps, --more--
but after that, you have our _really_ good wishes.

You _almost_ survived that poisoned arrow --more--
but your immunity from poison had gotten too low. --more--
"Entropy sucks", we know! DYWYPI?

Ah, NetHack; too bad there's not a "good -- evil"
axis. I could do _so_ well as the Avatar of Evil.

